In a Spring Boot controller, the most commonly used annotations are:

Class Level
@RestController

Used to create REST APIs. Combines @Controller + @ResponseBody.

@RestController
public class UserController {
}
@RequestMapping

Defines the base URL for all endpoints in the controller.

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {
}
Method Level Mapping Annotations
@GetMapping

For HTTP GET requests.

@GetMapping("/{id}")
public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userService.getUser(id);
}
@PostMapping

For HTTP POST requests.

@PostMapping
public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
    return userService.save(user);
}
@PutMapping

For HTTP PUT requests.

@PutMapping("/{id}")
public User updateUser(@PathVariable Long id,
                       @RequestBody User user) {
    return userService.update(id, user);
}
@DeleteMapping

For HTTP DELETE requests.

@DeleteMapping("/{id}")
public void deleteUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    userService.delete(id);
}
Generic @RequestMapping
@RequestMapping(value = "/test", method = RequestMethod.GET)
public String test() {
    return "Success";
}
Parameter Annotations
@PathVariable

Extract value from URL path.

@GetMapping("/{id}")
public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
}

URL:

GET /users/10
@RequestParam

Extract query parameter.

@GetMapping
public List<User> getUsers(
        @RequestParam String name,
        @RequestParam(required = false) Integer age) {
}

URL:

GET /users?name=Ali&age=25
@RequestBody

Maps JSON request body to Java object.

@PostMapping
public User save(@RequestBody User user) {
}

Request:

{
  "name": "Ali",
  "age": 25
}
@RequestHeader

Read request headers.

@GetMapping
public String getToken(
        @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {
}
@CookieValue

Read cookie values.

@GetMapping
public String getCookie(
        @CookieValue("sessionId") String sessionId) {
}
Response Related
@ResponseStatus
@PostMapping
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
public User create(@RequestBody User user) {
}

Returns HTTP 201 Created.

Validation
@Valid
@PostMapping
public User create(@Valid @RequestBody UserDto dto) {
}

DTO:

public class UserDto {

    @NotBlank
    private String name;

    @Email
    private String email;
}
Interview Question

Difference between @PathVariable and @RequestParam?

@GetMapping("/users/{id}")      // PathVariable

URL:

/users/10
@GetMapping("/users")           // RequestParam

URL:

/users?id=10
@PathVariable → Part of URL path.
@RequestParam → Query parameter.

These are the controller annotations most frequently asked in Spring Boot interviews.